{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01U6W2xWJ1gX5Yar1YQk86P8","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Verification Report — Round 7\n     2→\n     3→## Candidate Summary\n     4→- psi = -log(1 + x + ct) - log(1 + ct)\n     5→- alpha = 1\n     6→- s = -1/100\n     7→- lambda = -1/20\n     8→- Other params: none\n     9→\n    10→## Condition Results\n    11→\n    12→### Necessary Condition 1: L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ\n    13→- Status: **True** (proved analytically; engine returned \"unknown\" due to sympy limitations)\n    14→- Condition 1a (L − L₁ ≥ 0): **True**\n    15→- Condition 1b (−L₁ − L ≥ 0): **True**\n    16→- Symbolic expression (1a): c²·(29(ct)² + 20(ct)x + 10x² + 58(ct) + 20x + 29) / (200·(ct+1)^(39/20)·(ct+x+1)^(39/20))\n    17→- Symbolic expression (1b): c²·(26(ct)² + 16(ct)x + 9x² + 52(ct) + 16x + 26) / (200·(ct+1)^(39/20)·(ct+x+1)^(39/20))\n    18→- Numerical sampling: 1a: min = 4.74×10⁻⁴ > 0 (likely_true); 1b: min = 4.17×10⁻⁴ > 0 (likely_true)\n    19→- Diagnosis: Both conditions are proved by showing the numerators are positive-definite quadratic forms on the domain u = ct ≥ 0, v = x ≥ 0.\n    20→\n    21→  **Proof for 1a:** The numerator is Q₁(u,v) = 29u² + 20uv + 10v² + 58u + 20v + 29. The quadratic form matrix M₁ = [[29, 10], [10, 10]] has determinant 190 > 0 and leading entry 29 > 0, so it is positive definite. The unconstrained global minimum is at (u,v) = (-1, 0) with Q₁ = 0, which lies outside the domain u ≥ 0. On the boundary u = 0: Q₁ = 10v² + 20v + 29 = 10(v+1)² + 19 > 0. On the boundary v = 0: Q₁ = 29u² + 58u + 29 = 29(u+1)² > 0. At the corner (0,0): Q₁ = 29, with ∂Q₁/∂u = 58 > 0 and ∂Q₁/∂v = 20 > 0, so Q₁ is strictly increasing in both directions. Therefore Q₁ > 0 for all u ≥ 0, v ≥ 0. The denominator (ct+1)^(39/20)·(ct+x+1)^(39/20) > 0 and c² > 0, so condition 1a holds.\n    22→\n    23→  **Proof for 1b:** The numerator is Q₂(u,v) = 26u² + 16uv + 9v² + 52u + 16v + 26. The quadratic form matrix M₂ = [[26, 8], [8, 9]] has determinant 170 > 0 and leading entry 26 > 0, so it is positive definite. The unconstrained global minimum is at (u,v) = (-1, 0) with Q₂ = 0, outside the domain. On the boundary u = 0: Q₂ = 9v² + 16v + 26 > 0 (discriminant 256 - 936 < 0). On the boundary v = 0: Q₂ = 26u² + 52u + 26 = 26(u+1)² > 0. At (0,0): Q₂ = 26 with ∂Q₂/∂u = 52 > 0, ∂Q₂/∂v = 16 > 0. Therefore Q₂ > 0 for all u ≥ 0, v ≥ 0. ∎\n    24→\n    25→### Necessary Condition 2: lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞\n    26→- Status: **True** (confirmed symbolically by engine)\n    27→- λψ = (1/20)·(log(1 + x + ct) + log(1 + ct))\n    28→- Limit value: +∞\n    29→- Diagnosis: Since λ = -1/20 < 0 and ψ = -log(1+x+ct) - log(1+ct) → -∞ as x → ∞, we get λψ = (1/20)·log(1+x+ct) + (1/20)·log(1+ct) → +∞. The log(1+x+ct) term diverges while the log(1+ct) term is a positive constant in x. ∎\n    30→\n    31→### Necessary Condition 3: L₂ψ ≥ 0\n    32→- Status: **True** (proved analytically; engine returned \"unknown\" due to sympy limitations)\n    33→- Symbolic expression: L₂ = c⁴·N / (80000000000·(ct+1)^(79/20)·(ct+x+1)^(59/20)), where N is detailed below\n    34→- Numerical sampling: min = 4.97×10⁻⁹ > 0 (likely_true); verified with mpmath at (ct, x) = (10²⁰⁰⁰, 10²⁰⁰⁰): L₂ > 0\n    35→- Diagnosis: The denominator is manifestly positive. The numerator N is:\n    36→\n    37→  N = 212u³R + 12872500u³ + 252u²vR + 27887500u²v + 636u²R + 38617500u²\n    38→    + 143uv²R + 20767500uv² + 504uvR + 55775000uv + 636uR + 38617500u\n    39→    + 29v³R + 5752500v³ + 143v²R + 20767500v² + 252vR + 27887500v + 212R + 12872500\n    40→\n    41→  where u = ct ≥ 0, v = x ≥ 0, and R = (ct+1)^(1/10)·(ct+x+1)^(1/10) > 0.\n    42→\n    43→  **Proof:** Every term in N is a product of: (1) a positive integer coefficient, (2) a non-negative monomial u^a·v^b with a,b ≥ 0, and (3) either R > 0 or 1. Since u ≥ 0 and v ≥ 0, every monomial u^a·v^b ≥ 0. The constant term 212R + 12872500 ≥ 212·1 + 12872500 > 0 (since R ≥ 1 when ct ≥ 0, x ≥ 0). Therefore N is a sum of non-negative terms with at least one strictly positive term, hence N > 0 for all u ≥ 0, v ≥ 0, c > 0.\n    44→\n    45→  Confirmed numerically with mpmath (50+ digits) at extreme points including (ct, x) = (10²⁰⁰⁰, 10²⁰⁰⁰). ∎\n    46→\n    47→### Sufficient Condition: (L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(φ_xt)²\n    48→- Status: **True** (proved analytically; engine returned \"unknown\" due to sympy limitations)\n    49→- Symbolic expression: (L₁² − L² − c²φ_xt²) / φ² = c⁴·S / (160000·(ct+1)⁴·(ct+x+1)⁴), where:\n    50→  S = 2692u⁴ + 3972u³v + 10768u³ + 3363u²v² + 11916u²v + 16152u²\n    51→    + 1360uv³ + 6726uv² + 11916uv + 10768u + 360v⁴ + 1360v³ + 3363v² + 3972v + 2692\n    52→  with u = ct, v = x.\n    53→- Numerical sampling: min = 1.98×10⁻⁷ > 0 (likely_true); verified with mpmath at (ct, x) = (10²⁰⁰⁰, 10²⁰⁰⁰): S > 0\n    54→- Diagnosis:\n    55→\n    56→  **Proof:** The denominator 160000·(ct+1)⁴·(ct+x+1)⁴ > 0, φ² > 0, and c⁴ > 0. We need to show S > 0 for u ≥ 0, v ≥ 0.\n    57→\n    58→  All 15 coefficients in S are strictly positive: {2692, 3972, 10768, 3363, 11916, 16152, 1360, 6726, 11916, 10768, 360, 1360, 3363, 3972, 2692}. Since u ≥ 0 and v ≥ 0, every monomial u^a·v^b ≥ 0. The constant term is 2692 > 0. Therefore S is a sum of non-negative terms with at least one strictly positive term, so S > 0 for all u ≥ 0, v ≥ 0.\n    59→\n    60→  Confirmed numerically with mpmath at all extreme test points. ∎\n    61→\n    62→## Overall Summary\n    63→- Necessary condition 1: **True** (proved: positive-definite quadratic forms)\n    64→- Necessary condition 2: **True** (proved: symbolic limit)\n    65→- Necessary condition 3: **True** (proved: all coefficients positive with non-negative monomials)\n    66→- All necessary conditions: **True**\n    67→- Sufficient condition: **True** (proved: all coefficients positive with non-negative monomials)\n    68→- **ALL CONDITIONS PASS: True**\n    69→\n    70→## Failure Analysis\n    71→\n    72→No conditions failed. All